 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "THE GIANT'S CAUSEWAY. About 40,000 basalt columns, most of them hexagons, formed some 60 million years ago when lava cooled and cracked. A World Heritage Site since 1986. The columns don't care."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "FIONN AND BENANDONNER. Fionn mac Cumhaill built the causeway to reach Scotland and fight the giant Benandonner. Seeing how big his rival was, Fionn's wife dressed him as a baby. The rival fled."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "THE WISHING CHAIR. A natural seat of columns, said to grant a wish to anyone who sits on it. Don't wish for dry weather. It has a hard time with those."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "THE GRAND CAUSEWAY. The columns step down into the sea and carry on under the waves. On a calm day it feels like the beginning of a road somewhere. People have been following it for thousands of years."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "THE COASTAL PATH. West to Bushmills, a few miles by foot and a few minutes by train. The path is level. The weather is not."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 27,
   "plaque": "causeway_girona",
   "title": "Port na Spaniagh",
   "text": "In October 1588 the Girona, a galleass of the Spanish Armada, was wrecked at Lacada Point near the Causeway, and most of the 1,300 men aboard were lost. The bay is still called Port na Spaniagh. Her gold and jewels are now in the Ulster Museum."
  },
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 31,
   "plaque": "causeway_susanna_drury",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Susanna Drury",
   "text": "Around 1740 a Dublin artist, Susanna Drury, painted two views of the Causeway that won a prize from the Royal Dublin Society. Engravings of them spread round Europe, and scientists began arguing over how the columns formed. The columns kept quiet."
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "landsky_atlantic_opens",
   "title": "When the Atlantic opened",
   "text": "About 60 million years ago the Earth's crust began to tear between Europe and Greenland and the North Atlantic was born. Molten rock poured up through the tear and flooded Antrim, Mull, Skye and Staffa with the same basalt, which is why Fingal's Cave on Staffa has the same six-sided columns as the Causeway. Lava that cools slowly shrinks evenly and cracks into columns, as drying mud cracks into plates. The red band in the cliffs is old soil: a warm, wet pause between two flows."
  }
 ],
